import"./NZTpNUN0.js";import"./BkvxQF8z.js";import{t as i,P as n,q as r}from"./D3cAVrQp.js";var o=i('<h1>Migration and Human Mobility Framework v1.1.2</h1> <h2>Human Mobility, Displacement, Asylum, Reception, Return, and Host–Community Coordination</h2> <p><strong>Status:</strong> Controlled person-continuity successor; security/coercion release compatibility remains explicitly bounded<br> <strong>Version:</strong> 1.1.2<br> <strong>Supersedes:</strong> Migration and Human Mobility Framework v1.1.1 for future adoption; v1.0 remains historical source<br> <strong>Tier:</strong> Application framework<br> <strong>Date:</strong> 2026-08-08<br> <strong>Source preservation:</strong> The source v1.0 file remains unchanged.</p> <h3>Controlling interfaces</h3> <ul><li>Security, Coercion, and Protective Action Interface Specification v0.1 (<code>SCPA/0.1</code>);</li> <li>Justice Systems Framework v1.4.1;</li> <li>Constitutional Interface Specification v0.2.4 (<code>CIS/0.2.4</code>);</li> <li>Aurora Accord v1.3.2.</li></ul> <h3>Version history</h3> <ul><li><strong>1.1.2 (2026-08-08):</strong> Person-continuity and purpose-limited-attestation conformance patch to Justice v1.4.1, CIS/0.2.4, and Aurora v1.3.2. Preserves v1.1.1’s separation of civil identity, service access, protection status, residence/work authorization, travel documents, benefits, health, child protection, family tracing, and security records while adding explicit non-transitive same-person continuity, merge/split/duplicate/collision correction, source-preserving cross-domain linkage, and externally established death/status handling. Community attestation remains evidence rather than sovereign status; service credentials remain purpose-limited. Migration does not register death, determine general legal identity, citizenship, nationality, family status, legal capacity, heirship, or succession. Active cases, protection duties, family-tracing responsibilities, property claims, complaints, appeals, wages, benefits, and unresolved duties survive until competent law resolves them. <strong>Security/coercion compatibility is not silently re-certified:</strong> SCCA/0.1 was tested against Migration v1.1.1. v1.1.2 preserves those security-route semantics but requires an explicit registry compatibility update before SCCA may list v1.1.2 as an exact tested member.</li> <li><strong>Editorial edition (2026-08-02).</strong> No substantive change. Added this version history, which the document previously lacked; entries below are reconstructed from the document’s own text, and earlier editions remain to be recorded. Converted the inline controlling-interfaces sentence into a list matching the rest of the cluster, and added a Date line, which was missing. In the Appendix E manifest, the source-file name for v1.0 was a local download-artifact name (<code>v1.0(2).md</code>) and now uses the canonical name; the digest is unchanged and remains valid, as content hashes are name-independent. Digests are unchanged: they are load-bearing here, since the fail-closed rules depend on exact version identity.</li> <li><strong>1.1.1 (2026-08-01):</strong> Controlled conformance patch. Normatively adopts <code>SCPA-RIC/0.1</code>, <code>CFPAE/0.1</code>, <code>ESDIB/0.1</code>, and the exact security-and-coercion cluster release set <code>SCCA/0.1</code>, with digests recorded in the cluster-harmonization appendix. Changes interface adoption, record inheritance, transport, cross-framework responsibility, emergency linkage, pairwise specialization, and exact release compatibility. Adds no police, intelligence, investigation, search, custody, force, cyber, border, sanctions, or prosecutorial authority. Documented at Appendix F.</li> <li><strong>1.1:</strong> Constitutional revision of the Enhanced Global Governance Framework for Migration and Human Mobility v1.0. Preserved the source’s orientation toward dignity over documentation, the right to move and the right to stay, anti-criminalization, unconditional support, family unity, and trauma-informed practice, while declining to treat its proposed institutions, passports, biometric systems, protected zones, automatic visas, status categories, guardianship arrangements, corridors, and funding mechanisms as already-constituted legal powers. Separated immediate service access, civil identity and identity reconstruction, asylum and protection status, residence and work authorization, travel documentation and border admission, and reception and placement into distinct decisions. Removed the source’s self-executing effects: automatic climate visas, temporary protected personhood, biometric Love Ledger enrolment, universal mobility credentials, protected mobility zones, guardian assignment, deportation prevention, relocation rights, and their attached coercive consequences.</li> <li><strong>Earlier editions:</strong> v1.0 remains the historical source; its history is not yet recorded in this document.</li></ul> <blockquote><p><strong>Dignity does not depend on documentation. Protection does not create border authority. Mobility status does not create dangerousness. No valid authority record, no consequential mobility or coercive action.</strong></p></blockquote> <hr> <h1>1. Revision basis</h1> <h2>1.1 Source freeze</h2> <p>This document succeeds the Enhanced Global Governance Framework for Migration & Human Mobility v1.0. It preserves the source framework’s orientation toward dignity over documentation, the right to move and the right to stay, anti-criminalization, unconditional support, family unity, trauma-informed reception, host–migrant co-governance, right of return, rematriation, and non-punitive solidarity.</p> <p>It does not silently treat the source’s proposed institutions, passports, biometric systems, protected zones, automatic visas, status categories, guardianship arrangements, corridors, or funding mechanisms as already constituted legal powers.</p> <h2>1.2 Constitutional problem addressed</h2> <p>The source combined humane objectives with several self-executing legal effects: automatic climate visas, temporary protected personhood, biometric Love Ledger enrolment, universal mobility credentials, protected mobility zones, guardian assignment, deportation prevention, relocation rights, and territorial arrangements. Those objectives may be legitimate when enacted through competent law, but the framework itself cannot create jurisdiction, legal status, border access, custody, identity, guardianship, removal, or return authority.</p> <p>v1.1 therefore separates:</p> <ol><li>immediate service access;</li> <li>civil identity and identity reconstruction;</li> <li>asylum and protection status;</li> <li>residence and work authorization;</li> <li>travel documentation and border admission;</li> <li>reception and placement;</li> <li>family tracing and guardianship;</li> <li>detention and release;</li> <li>expulsion, deportation, extradition, and transfer;</li> <li>voluntary return, right of return, and rematriation;</li> <li>planned relocation and climate mobility;</li> <li>host-community coordination and Indigenous territorial authority.</li></ol> <h2>1.3 What is preserved</h2> <p>The successor preserves and strengthens:</p> <ul><li>dignity over documentation;</li> <li>protection and essential services without compulsory identity enrolment;</li> <li>a right to seek safety and a right to remain where viable;</li> <li>non-refoulement and protection against return to grave harm;</li> <li>family unity and child best interests;</li> <li>freedom from arbitrary or routine immigration detention;</li> <li>survivor-centred trafficking and exploitation responses;</li> <li>unconditional support without assimilation milestones;</li> <li>host-community participation without punishment for non-participation;</li> <li>Indigenous nation-specific authority over affected territories;</li> <li>trauma-informed, accessible reception;</li> <li>right to voluntary return, property claims, cultural reconnection, and rematriation;</li> <li>anti-gentrification, labour, housing, and community-infrastructure safeguards;</li> <li>no use of mobility status as a proxy for criminality, threat, worth, or belonging.</li></ul> <h2>1.4 What is retired or narrowed</h2> <p>v1.1 retires or narrows:</p> <ul><li>a universal Global Mobility Passport;</li> <li>mandatory blockchain or Love Ledger participation;</li> <li>compulsory biometric enrolment;</li> <li>automatic visa or status creation from environmental metrics;</li> <li>framework-created Protected Mobility Zones;</li> <li>framework-created global guardianship authority;</li> <li>automatic identity documents without competent issuers;</li> <li>security screening merged with service access;</li> <li>“successful integration” as a funding or eligibility metric;</li> <li>fixed host-population thresholds that automatically create governance duties;</li> <li>Reciprocity Capital as a tradable claim on public finance;</li> <li>automatic land-tenure grants or governance transfers by framework declaration;</li> <li>any GGF body acting as border police, detention authority, deportation authority, or enforcement service merely because it supports migrants.</li></ul> <hr> <h1>2. Purpose, scope, and non-authority</h1> <h2>2.1 Purpose</h2> <p>The framework helps competent authorities, communities, service providers, migrants, displaced persons, stateless persons, refugees, asylum seekers, survivors, workers, families, and affected Indigenous nations coordinate humane mobility systems while preserving rights, lawful jurisdiction, local capacity, and meaningful choice.</p> <h2>2.2 Scope</h2> <p>The framework covers:</p> <ul><li>displacement prevention and the right to stay;</li> <li>emergency reception and essential services;</li> <li>de facto displacement recognition for service access;</li> <li>identity reconstruction and documentation support;</li> <li>asylum, temporary protection, and non-refoulement;</li> <li>family tracing, reunification, and child protection;</li> <li>trafficking, exploitation, and labour protection;</li> <li>planned relocation and climate mobility;</li> <li>host–migrant co-governance;</li> <li>voluntary return, return rights, and rematriation;</li> <li>legal defence, complaint, appeal, and remedy;</li> <li>interfaces with safety, crime cooperation, peacebuilding, disaster response, health, data governance, work, and Indigenous governance.</li></ul> <h2>2.3 Non-authority clause</h2> <p>This framework does not itself:</p> <ul><li>create citizenship, nationality, residence, asylum, refugee, visa, work, travel, or protection status;</li> <li>authorize admission, exclusion, border search, interception, detention, deportation, extradition, transfer, removal, return, or relocation;</li> <li>create a border, protected zone, corridor, camp, reception centre, guardianship, tribunal, police body, intelligence service, or enforcement unit;</li> <li>issue identity documents or travel credentials;</li> <li>compel biometric collection, enrolment, data sharing, health screening, labour, relocation, assimilation, return, or participation in a digital ledger;</li> <li>transfer territory, land, jurisdiction, or Indigenous authority;</li> <li>authorize sanctions, asset restraint, prosecution, or corporate enforcement;</li> <li>override non-refoulement, family unity, child best interests, disability rights, survivor choice, Indigenous authority, or independent review.</li></ul> <p>Every consequential act requires an independently valid constituting instrument and the applicable SCPA record bundle.</p> <h2>2.4 Normative language</h2> <p>“Shall” identifies a requirement for framework conformance. “Should” identifies a strong presumption requiring reasons for departure. “May” identifies permission within independently valid authority. Nothing in this document converts a recommendation into legal power.</p> <hr> <h1>3. Core principles</h1> <h2>3.1 Dignity over documentation</h2> <p>Lack of documents may complicate identity or status adjudication. It shall not by itself bar emergency shelter, food, water, urgent healthcare, interpretation, disability support, protection from violence, child safeguarding, legal information, or access to a protection procedure.</p> <h2>3.2 Protection and status are distinct</h2> <p>A person may receive immediate protection and services without a final legal-status decision. Temporary assistance shall not be misrepresented as citizenship, residence, asylum, or permanent admission.</p> <h2>3.3 Right to move and right to stay</h2> <p>Mobility policy shall support both safe movement and the ability to remain in place with dignity. Adaptation, restoration, livelihood, peacebuilding, and disaster-risk reduction shall not be neglected in order to make relocation appear inevitable.</p> <h2>3.4 Non-refoulement</h2> <p>No person shall be transferred, expelled, extradited, deported, rejected, informally pushed back, or induced to return where there is a substantial risk of persecution, torture, enforced disappearance, arbitrary killing, trafficking, severe gender-based harm, catastrophic deprivation, or other grave and legally protected harm. The applicable legal standard and competent adjudicator must be recorded.</p> <h2>3.5 Individualized decision and prohibited inference</h2> <p>Nationality, ethnicity, religion, language, disability, migration route, documentation gap, homelessness, poverty, biometric refusal, community affiliation, asylum claim, political opinion, trauma response, or presence in a high-risk area shall not by themselves establish fraud, dangerousness, criminality, security threat, lack of credibility, or ineligibility.</p> <h2>3.6 Family unity</h2> <p>Authorities shall avoid separation, actively trace missing family members where safe, and make any separation decision through a lawful, individualized, reviewable process. Family unity does not override survivor safety or a child’s best interests.</p> <h2>3.7 Child best interests and evolving capacity</h2> <p>Children are rights-holders. Best-interests assessment, independent representation, evolving capacity, family tracing, education, healthcare, and safe care shall be operational requirements rather than aspirational language.</p> <h2>3.8 Disability and accessibility</h2> <p>Communication, transport, shelter, procedures, hearings, documentation, and services shall be accessible. Support persons, sign language, alternative communication, quiet spaces, mobility equipment, medication continuity, and reasonable accommodations shall be available without adverse inference.</p> <h2>3.9 Survivor sovereignty</h2> <p>People affected by trafficking, exploitation, torture, sexual violence, coercive control, or organized crime retain choice over support, reporting, testimony, relocation, and restorative participation. Support shall not depend on cooperation with investigation or prosecution.</p> <h2>3.10 Indigenous affected-nation authority</h2> <p>No universal Indigenous council or proxy may authorize mobility arrangements for all Indigenous peoples. Entry, transit, reception, relocation, data collection, land use, rematriation, and return affecting an Indigenous nation require that nation’s own authority and procedures.</p> <h2>3.11 Host-community dignity without exclusionary power</h2> <p>Host communities are entitled to truthful capacity information, infrastructure support, participation, and remedy for imposed burdens. Participation does not create power to discriminate, collectively expel, deny essential services, or subordinate individual rights to plebiscitary preference.</p> <h2>3.12 No soft coercion</h2> <p>Funding, infrastructure, public recognition, mobility support, or cooperation shall not be conditioned on assimilation scores, political loyalty, biometric enrolment, intelligence sharing, acceptance of a fixed population quota, participation in a ledger, or waiver of rights.</p> <h2>3.13 Least restrictive effective measure</h2> <p>When a legitimate mobility objective can be achieved through reporting support, case management, community placement, documentation assistance, scheduling, financial assistance, or another less restrictive measure, custody, surveillance, confinement, exclusion, or compulsory movement shall not be used.</p> <h2>3.14 Complete lifecycle and remedy</h2> <p>Every consequential mobility action shall include notice, reasons, review, appeal, termination, correction, data disposition, release or handback where applicable, and remedy for wrongful action.</p> <hr> <h1>4. SCPA action classes and functional separation</h1> <h2>4.1 Ordinary framework activity</h2> <p>Most Migration v1.1.1 activity belongs to <strong>SCPA Class A: voluntary assistance and prevention</strong>. Reception, shelter, legal information, interpretation, voluntary casework, family tracing, host dialogue, livelihood support, and voluntary return assistance can be provided without coercive authority when participation is genuinely voluntary and data use is bounded.</p> <h2>4.2 Actions requiring separate routes</h2> <p>The following require separate SCPA routes:</p> <table><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>SCPA class</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>observation and protected warning</td><td>B</td><td>aggregate displacement monitoring; route-safety warning</td></tr><tr><td>investigation</td><td>C</td><td>trafficking investigation; document fraud inquiry</td></tr><tr><td>urgent protection</td><td>D</td><td>immediate evacuation from trafficking or violence</td></tr><tr><td>compulsory process</td><td>E</td><td>compulsory disclosure; search; document seizure</td></tr><tr><td>custody and force</td><td>F</td><td>detention; restraint; involuntary transport</td></tr><tr><td>cyber intervention</td><td>G</td><td>defensive action on a reception system; third-party disruption</td></tr><tr><td>financial coercion</td><td>H</td><td>sanctions; asset freezes; forfeiture</td></tr><tr><td>adjudication and remedy</td><td>I</td><td>asylum determination; detention review; appeal</td></tr><tr><td>reintegration</td><td>J</td><td>voluntary return and restorative reintegration</td></tr><tr><td>mobility and territorial action</td><td>K</td><td>admission; visa; status; border action; expulsion; corridor; relocation</td></tr></tbody></table> <h2>4.3 No upward inference</h2> <p>A service-access record is not an identity determination. Identity evidence is not a security allegation. A security allegation is not an asylum exclusion. An asylum exclusion is not a detention order. A detention order is not a deportation order. A deportation order is not an extradition decision. A relocation offer is not consent. A host compact is not territorial jurisdiction.</p> <h2>4.4 Referral is not transfer of authority</h2> <p>A mobility service may refer a protection concern to a competent authority. It may not accept legal responsibility on that authority’s behalf, command an investigation, determine guilt, order detention, or execute removal.</p> <hr> <h1>5. Institutional architecture</h1> <h2>5.1 No global mobility sovereign</h2> <p>v1.1 creates no global ministry, passport authority, border command, asylum tribunal, detention service, or territorial administration.</p> <h2>5.2 Federated Mobility Support and Reception Network</h2> <p>Participating institutions may form a non-sovereign network to:</p> <ul><li>publish model service standards;</li> <li>coordinate voluntary assistance;</li> <li>route requests and offers;</li> <li>maintain interpreters and accessible information;</li> <li>support family tracing;</li> <li>develop reception and host-infrastructure tools;</li> <li>facilitate peer learning;</li> <li>provide capacity grants through separately valid finance;</li> <li>publish aggregate, privacy-protective learning.</li></ul> <p>The network may not issue legal status, identity documents, travel documents, admission decisions, detention orders, removal orders, or territorial permissions.</p> <h2>5.3 Legal Protection and Casework Services</h2> <p>Independent or publicly chartered casework services should provide:</p> <ul><li>legal orientation and referral;</li> <li>access to counsel;</li> <li>protection application support;</li> <li>identity reconstruction assistance;</li> <li>family tracing;</li> <li>disability and communication support;</li> <li>trafficking and survivor advocacy;</li> <li>complaints and urgent-relief applications.</li></ul> <p>Caseworkers are not intelligence collectors or adjudicators.</p> <h2>5.4 Host–Migrant Compact Facilitation</h2> <p>Neutral facilitators may help affected residents, incoming populations, local authorities, service providers, workers, landlords, schools, health systems, and affected Indigenous nations negotiate infrastructure and coexistence arrangements. A compact cannot waive non-derogable rights or create detention, exclusion, surveillance, taxation, or removal authority.</p> <h2>5.5 Independent Mobility Ombudsperson</h2> <p>Each implementation shall provide an ombuds or equivalent complaint route independent of reception operators, status adjudicators, detention operators, employers, landlords, and security services. The ombuds may investigate administration, support urgent referrals, recommend correction, and publish aggregate findings. It does not replace courts or legally competent review bodies.</p> <hr> <h1>6. Access, attestation, and immediate assistance</h1> <h2>6.1 Minimum attestation</h2> <p>A person seeking immediate help may use self-attestation, community attestation, humanitarian referral, family confirmation, prior records, witness evidence, digital or paper copies, or other reasonable evidence. The threshold for essential services shall be lower than the threshold for permanent legal identity or status.</p> <p>Any identifier or attestation used at this stage is purpose-limited to the specific service, continuity, or casework function. It does not create general civil identity, citizenship, nationality, migration status, family status, voting eligibility, or legal personhood.</p> <h2>6.2 Community attestation</h2> <p>Community attestation is evidence, not sovereign status creation. Its weight depends on purpose, reliability, potential conflicts, and challenge rights. Fraud safeguards shall target deliberate deception rather than punish people whose records were destroyed, withheld, inconsistent, translated differently, or never issued.</p> <h2>6.3 Service-access credential</h2> <p>Where a credential is useful, it shall be purpose-limited to service access and shall not silently become a travel document, national identity, criminal identifier, general benefits key, employment identity, healthcare identity, justice identity, voting credential, reputation score, universal authentication token, or security credential.</p> <p>The credential should state or make discoverable its issuer, purpose, authority, evidence class and uncertainty, permitted relying process, prohibited uses, validity/review condition, correction/challenge route, and whether any cross-domain continuity recognition is prohibited or separately authorized.</p> <h2>6.4 No mandatory identity before aid</h2> <p>Emergency shelter, food, urgent care, interpretation, disability support, protection from violence, and child safeguarding shall not depend on biometric enrolment, a blockchain account, a Love Ledger account, or possession of a passport.</p> <h2>6.5 Exit and refusal</h2> <p>A person may refuse optional enrolment, case management, cultural programming, work placement, spiritual support, or community activities without losing essential assistance or attracting a security flag.</p> <hr> <h1>7. Identity, credentials, and documentation</h1> <h2>7.1 Functional separation</h2> <p>The following functions shall be separated unless competent law expressly joins them with adequate safeguards:</p> <ol><li>civil identity;</li> <li>service access;</li> <li>asylum or protection case;</li> <li>residence authorization;</li> <li>work authorization;</li> <li>travel document;</li> <li>benefit account;</li> <li>health record;</li> <li>education record;</li> <li>child-protection record;</li> <li>family-tracing record;</li> <li>security or criminal-investigation record.</li></ol> <h2>7.2 Identity reconstruction</h2> <p>Identity reconstruction shall use plural evidence and record uncertainty. It shall permit correction and later supplementation. A person shall not be forced to assert a nationality, family relation, date of birth, or name that they reasonably contest.</p> <h2>7.3 Biometrics</h2> <p>Biometrics are not the default condition of dignity or service. Any biometric use requires:</p> <ul><li>a specific lawful purpose;</li> <li>proof that a less intrusive alternative is inadequate;</li> <li>informed notice and accessible explanation;</li> <li>a rights-equivalent non-biometric route where feasible;</li> <li>separation from criminal and intelligence databases;</li> <li>strict access, retention, correction, and deletion rules;</li> <li>independent review and remedy;</li> <li>additional safeguards for children, survivors, and people whose bodies or identities place them at risk.</li></ul> <p>Biometric refusal shall not itself establish fraud, dangerousness, or ineligibility.</p> <h2>7.4 No universal mobility passport</h2> <p>A future interoperable credential may support voluntary travel or service portability only when issued by competent authorities and governed by Aurora Accord v1.3.2 and CIS/0.2.4. v1.1.2 does not create a universal passport, identity number, master identity graph, or global population registry.</p> <h2>7.5 Lost, destroyed, or withheld documents</h2> <p>Officials shall consider conflict, disaster, statelessness, persecution, family control, trafficking, discrimination, non-registration, and administrative collapse when documents are missing. Negative credibility findings require reasons beyond the absence of papers.</p> <h2>7.6 Person continuity across mobility and service records</h2> <p>Where two Migration records, or a Migration record and another domain record, are asserted to concern the same person, continuity recognition follows CIS/0.2.4 and Aurora v1.3.2.</p> <p>Before consequential reliance, the responsible body records:</p> <ul><li>the exact records being related;</li> <li>the specific mobility, protection, service, family-tracing, return, or continuity purpose;</li> <li>the authority permitting the relation;</li> <li>minimum necessary attributes;</li> <li>source provenance, uncertainty, and conflicting evidence;</li> <li>affected-person notice where lawful and safe;</li> <li>correction, mistaken-merge, mistaken-split, duplicate, and collision routes;</li> <li>challenge/review;</li> <li>expiry, unlinking, or renewed-authorization conditions.</li></ul> <p>Same-person recognition is <strong>non-transitive by default</strong>. A lawful link between Migration and Housing, and a separate link between Housing and AUBI, does not authorize Migration to access AUBI records or infer benefit status.</p> <p>A continuity assertion links bounded records for a stated purpose; it does not merge source records, establish nationality or family relation, or create a universal identifier.</p> <h2>7.7 External civil-status changes and unresolved duties</h2> <p>Migration may receive a death record, legal-name correction, guardianship change, corrected civil record, or another status transition established by a competent external authority where that event is relevant to an active mobility, protection, family, service, return, property, or remedy process.</p> <p>Migration:</p> <ul><li>records the source authority, effective date, and material contest or uncertainty;</li> <li>applies only consequences within its own lawful mandate;</li> <li>does not register the death or infer it from disappearance, non-response, document expiry, account dormancy, missed reporting, service non-use, or missing telemetry;</li> <li>does not determine heirs, probate, family status, legal capacity, citizenship, nationality, or succession entitlement merely by receiving the event;</li> <li>preserves active protection questions, complaints, appeals, family-tracing responsibilities, property/return claims, wages, benefits, data-correction rights, remedies, and unresolved duties until competent law resolves survival, substitution, succession, or closure;</li> <li>uses CIS/0.2.4 for person continuity and GMEAIA/SCPA/CFPAE lifecycle machinery where those interfaces apply.</li></ul> <p>Death may alter a case or service relationship under applicable law. It does not automatically erase what the person was owed, what others still owe, or what must be corrected.</p> <hr> <h1>8. Asylum, protection, and non-refoulement</h1> <h2>8.1 Access to procedure</h2> <p>Any person who expresses fear of return, requests protection, or presents indicators of trafficking or grave harm shall receive access to a competent protection procedure and shall not be removed before that claim is assessed, subject only to narrowly lawful and reviewable exceptions consistent with non-refoulement.</p> <h2>8.2 Registration is not adjudication</h2> <p>Initial registration records a request and protects access. It does not determine credibility, nationality, exclusion, security risk, or final status.</p> <h2>8.3 Temporary admission or protection</h2> <p>Competent law may provide temporary admission, humanitarian stay, disaster protection, or other provisional status. Environmental or conflict indicators may trigger assessment and preparedness, but metrics alone do not issue a visa or create legal status.</p> <h2>8.4 Evidence</h2> <p>Protection adjudication shall accept testimony, country and territory information, community evidence, medical or psychological evidence with consent, digital evidence, documentary evidence, and reasonable inference. It shall account for trauma, translation, memory, disability, age, cultural context, concealment needed for survival, and documentation loss.</p> <h2>8.5 Counsel, interpretation, and accessibility</h2> <p>Applicants shall receive understandable notice, competent interpretation, access to counsel or qualified assistance, disability accommodation, and sufficient time to present material facts.</p> <h2>8.6 Confidentiality</h2> <p>Protection information shall not be disclosed to alleged persecutors, hostile authorities, employers, landlords, family members, community leaders, or security actors without consent or a specific lawful basis, necessity, minimization, and independent authorization.</p> <h2>8.7 Country and territory information</h2> <p>Country or territory information shall be plural, current, contestable, and transparent about uncertainty. Automated country-risk scoring shall not decide an individual case.</p> <h2>8.8 Exclusion and serious allegations</h2> <p>Exclusion from protection requires lawful criteria, individualized evidence, disclosure sufficient for meaningful challenge, and independent adjudication. Association, nationality, route, family relationship, or untested intelligence shall not suffice.</p> <h2>8.9 Security allegation firewall</h2> <p>A Shield or intelligence allegation may be relevant only through <code>SCPA-30</code>, with provenance, reliability, purpose limits, disclosure or a lawful substitute, independent review, and an opportunity to answer. It cannot automatically defeat asylum, justify detention, separate a family, or authorize removal.</p> <h2>8.10 Appeal and interim relief</h2> <p>A person shall have access to an effective appeal or review with suspensive effect where removal could cause irreparable harm. Urgent interim relief shall be available.</p> <h2>8.11 Cessation and revocation</h2> <p>Ending protection requires competent law, individualized reasons, changed circumstances that are durable and safe, notice, access to evidence, appeal, and non-refoulement review. Fraud allegations require proof and proportionality; they do not erase protection against return to grave harm.</p> <hr> <h1>9. Reception and the first 72 hours</h1> <h2>9.1 Reception is care, not custody</h2> <p>Reception facilities, shelters, camps, transit centres, and safe spaces shall be presumed non-carceral. Locking exits, confiscating mobility aids, coercive transport, constant armed control, enforced isolation, or inability to leave constitutes restraint or custody regardless of label and requires SCPA Class F authority.</p> <h2>9.2 First six hours</h2> <p>Priorities are immediate safety, urgent healthcare, water, food, weather protection, interpretation, accessibility, identification of separated children, and confidential screening for imminent protection needs. Screening is for routing support, not deciding credibility or threat status.</p> <h2>9.3 Six to twenty-four hours</h2> <p>Offer safe accommodation, communication with family or trusted persons, legal information, survivor support, disability accommodation, medication continuity, culturally appropriate food, child-safe spaces, and voluntary documentation assistance.</p> <h2>9.4 Twenty-four to seventy-two hours</h2> <p>Begin family tracing where safe, assign independent child representation where legally required, provide casework options, assess longer-term placement and host capacity, and document any unresolved urgent needs. Participation remains voluntary except where a separate lawful protective action applies.</p> <h2>9.5 Health and trauma</h2> <p>Trauma screening shall be optional, clinically appropriate, confidential, and separated from credibility or security assessment. Health data shall not be used for immigration enforcement except under a specific lawful basis meeting necessity and proportionality.</p> <h2>9.6 Reception conditions</h2> <p>Standards shall cover privacy, sanitation, safety, gender and family needs, disability access, communication, complaint routes, legal access, education, healthcare, freedom of movement, fire safety, food, cultural needs, and protection from exploitation.</p> <hr> <h1>10. Children, families, and dependent persons</h1> <h2>10.1 Family tracing and reunification</h2> <p>Tracing shall be consent-sensitive and safety-aware. Reunification shall not occur with an alleged abuser, trafficker, coercive controller, or unsafe custodian merely because a biological relationship is asserted.</p> <h2>10.2 Independent guardianship</h2> <p>A guardian for an unaccompanied or separated child must be appointed by competent law, independent of migration enforcement and reception operators, trained, conflict-checked, and reviewable. The framework does not appoint guardians.</p> <h2>10.3 Best-interests procedure</h2> <p>Major decisions affecting a child require a documented best-interests process, the child’s views according to evolving capacity, independent representation, family and safety assessment, reasons, and review.</p> <h2>10.4 No child immigration detention</h2> <p>Children shall not be detained for migration-control convenience. Families shall not be detained merely to avoid separation. Protective care that prevents exit or imposes coercive control must be legally classified and independently reviewed.</p> <h2>10.5 Education and continuity</h2> <p>Children should enter accessible education promptly without waiting for final status. Prior learning, language needs, disability, cultural continuity, and protection from discrimination shall be addressed.</p> <hr> <h1>11. Trafficking, exploitation, and survivor protection</h1> <h2>11.1 Identification is not prosecution</h2> <p>Indicators of trafficking or exploitation trigger offers of safety, legal advice, healthcare, accommodation, and survivor advocacy. They do not compel a criminal complaint or testimony.</p> <h2>11.2 Reflection and recovery</h2> <p>Competent law should provide a safe reflection period during which immediate needs are met and removal is stayed where necessary to protect the person and assess claims.</p> <h2>11.3 Non-punishment</h2> <p>Survivors should not be penalized for immigration, labour, document, sex-work, or low-level criminal conduct they were compelled to perform, subject to lawful individualized assessment.</p> <h2>11.4 Confidentiality and relocation</h2> <p>Relocation, identity protection, or witness support shall be voluntary where possible and independently authorized where coercive. Survivor location and identity shall not be exposed through public dashboards or host-community processes.</p> <h2>11.5 Labour recruitment</h2> <p>Recruiters and employers shall not confiscate documents, charge unlawful fees, tie status to a single abusive employer without exit, control housing as leverage, retaliate for complaints, or use debt to compel work.</p> <hr> <h1>12. Liberty, detention, and alternatives</h1> <h2>12.1 Presumption of liberty</h2> <p>Migration status does not by itself justify detention. Administrative convenience, deterrence, lack of documents, poverty, missed appointments without context, or inability to pay shall not suffice.</p> <h2>12.2 Lawful basis and individualized necessity</h2> <p>Any detention requires competent law, individualized necessity, proportionality, a legitimate purpose, consideration of less restrictive alternatives, written reasons, counsel, interpretation, welfare safeguards, and prompt independent review.</p> <h2>12.3 Less restrictive alternatives</h2> <p>Authorities shall consider voluntary case management, reporting support, reminders, travel assistance, community placement, guarantors, documentation support, and tailored supervision. Alternatives shall not become intrusive surveillance regimes or impossible financial conditions.</p> <h2>12.4 Review and duration</h2> <p>Detention shall be reviewed promptly and periodically by an independent body. Indefinite, unreviewed, punitive, secret, or automatic detention is prohibited. Release follows when the purpose cannot be achieved, removal is not reasonably foreseeable, conditions are harmful, or alternatives suffice.</p> <h2>12.5 Conditions and access</h2> <p>People in custody retain healthcare, legal access, communication, family contact, interpretation, disability support, religious freedom, complaint rights, protection from violence, and humane conditions. Detention operators may not obstruct counsel or review.</p> <h2>12.6 No detention outsourcing without responsibility</h2> <p>Contracting a private, foreign, local, humanitarian, or community entity does not remove the authorizing authority’s responsibility for legality, welfare, records, review, and remedy.</p> <hr> <h1>13. Expulsion, deportation, extradition, transfer, and return</h1> <h2>13.1 Separate legal acts</h2> <p>Expulsion, deportation, extradition, criminal transfer, Dublin-type responsibility transfer, voluntary return, assisted return, evacuation, and rematriation are distinct acts. One authorization cannot substitute for another.</p> <h2>13.2 Removal decision</h2> <p>A removal decision requires competent jurisdiction, individualized reasons, status and protection review, family and child assessment, health and disability safeguards, notice, counsel, interpretation, appeal, and an executable lawful destination.</p> <h2>13.3 Extradition</h2> <p>Extradition requires its own criminal-law process. An asylum refusal does not authorize extradition; an extradition request does not extinguish non-refoulement or asylum rights.</p> <h2>13.4 Diplomatic assurances</h2> <p>Assurances shall not replace an evidence-based risk assessment unless they are specific, credible, independently monitorable, legally meaningful, and subject to review.</p> <h2>13.5 Informal and indirect return</h2> <p>Pushbacks, carrier pressure, visa denial designed to evade protection duties, transfer through unsafe intermediaries, denial of disembarkation, coercive “voluntary” return, and chain refoulement are subject to the same rights constraints as formal removal.</p> <h2>13.6 Voluntary return</h2> <p>Return is voluntary only when informed, uncoerced, practically rejectable, and not induced by unlawful detention, destitution, family separation, threats, withdrawal of essential care, or misleading information.</p> <hr> <h1>14. Borders, corridors, protected routes, and territorial arrangements</h1> <h2>14.1 No framework-created zones</h2> <p>A Protected Mobility Zone, sanctuary jurisdiction, corridor, reception area, evacuation route, transit arrangement, or relocation site requires valid host-state, municipal, treaty, Indigenous, land, maritime, aviation, and other applicable authority. The framework cannot establish such territory by declaration.</p> <h2>14.2 Corridors</h2> <p>A corridor instrument shall identify:</p> <ul><li>legal basis and participating authorities;</li> <li>geographic and temporal scope;</li> <li>eligible uses and users;</li> <li>command and safety responsibilities;</li> <li>border and admission rules;</li> <li>data and screening boundaries;</li> <li>non-refoulement safeguards;</li> <li>humanitarian access;</li> <li>labour and accessibility requirements;</li> <li>termination and handback;</li> <li>complaint and remedy paths.</li></ul> <h2>14.3 Rescue and disembarkation</h2> <p>Rescue obligations, safe disembarkation, admission, status determination, and responsibility allocation are separate functions. Rescue must not be withheld to manipulate migration policy.</p> <h2>14.4 Sanctuary</h2> <p>A sanctuary policy is valid only within the adopting authority’s competence. It may protect access to services and limit voluntary cooperation with enforcement where lawful; it cannot erase another jurisdiction’s authority or create immunity from all law.</p> <h2>14.5 Indigenous transit and territory</h2> <p>Routes through Indigenous territory require the affected nation’s own authorization. Emergency life-saving entry without prior consent must remain narrow, necessary, time-limited, documented, reviewable, and followed by notification, restoration, and compensation where appropriate.</p> <hr> <h1>15. Climate mobility, disaster displacement, and planned relocation</h1> <h2>15.1 Metrics trigger assessment, not status</h2> <p>Heat, storm, sea-level, drought, habitability, conflict, or infrastructure indicators may trigger warning, assistance, adaptation planning, and a protection assessment. They do not automatically issue visas, transfer land, order relocation, or determine that an area is uninhabitable.</p> <h2>15.2 Right to stay</h2> <p>Before relocation, authorities shall assess in-situ adaptation, ecosystem restoration, infrastructure, livelihood, health, water, housing, and cultural-continuity options. People shall not be moved merely because relocation is cheaper for an institution.</p> <h2>15.3 Planned relocation</h2> <p>Any relocation programme requires:</p> <ul><li>a valid legal basis;</li> <li>meaningful participation;</li> <li>individual and collective consent standards appropriate to the context;</li> <li>affected Indigenous nation authority;</li> <li>safe housing, livelihoods, education, health, accessibility, and cultural continuity;</li> <li>property, land, compensation, and return arrangements;</li> <li>host-community infrastructure;</li> <li>independent review;</li> <li>a no-retaliation route for refusal;</li> <li>monitoring for exploitation, trafficking, and secondary displacement.</li></ul> <h2>15.4 Evacuation</h2> <p>Emergency evacuation under SCPA Class D must be distinguished from permanent relocation. People shall not lose property, status, land claims, or return rights because they accepted temporary rescue.</p> <h2>15.5 Slow-onset displacement</h2> <p>Slow-onset processes require periodic, plural, uncertainty-aware assessments rather than a single irreversible classification. Planning should preserve optionality and avoid depressing investment in places deemed “future sacrifice zones.”</p> <hr> <h1>16. Host–migrant co-governance and community capacity</h1> <h2>16.1 Compact purpose</h2> <p>A compact coordinates infrastructure, services, participation, conflict resolution, housing, education, healthcare, transport, work, public space, and cultural life. It is not a contract by which migrants surrender rights or hosts receive exclusion powers.</p> <h2>16.2 Representation</h2> <p>Incoming populations and host residents shall select representatives through accessible, plural methods. Women, children through appropriate representation, disabled people, workers, tenants, survivors, minorities, and affected Indigenous nations shall have meaningful participation.</p> <h2>16.3 No automatic population thresholds</h2> <p>Population ratios may inform planning but shall not automatically create duties, vetoes, emergency status, funding entitlement, or exclusion authority.</p> <h2>16.4 Host capacity</h2> <p>Capacity assessment shall cover housing, water, sanitation, schools, healthcare, transport, environment, labour markets, public administration, language access, disability services, safety, and social infrastructure. “Capacity” shall not be a disguised demographic preference or ethnic-exclusion tool.</p> <h2>16.5 Third spaces</h2> <p>Shared kitchens, gardens, cultural venues, sports facilities, childcare, learning centres, and neighbourhood commons may support voluntary relationship-building. Participation shall not be measured as assimilation or loyalty.</p> <h2>16.6 Conflict resolution</h2> <p>Peacebuilding and Kintsugi services may facilitate disputes voluntarily. Mediation records remain presumptively unavailable for immigration, intelligence, employment, benefit, or criminal decisions.</p> <hr> <h1>17. Right of return, voluntary return, and rematriation</h1> <h2>17.1 No universal fixed expiry</h2> <p>The source’s twenty-year or one-generation period may be useful as a planning benchmark but cannot universally determine legal rights. Return rights depend on applicable law, displacement circumstances, property, nationality, peace agreements, Indigenous authority, and continuing safety.</p> <h2>17.2 Return components</h2> <p>Return frameworks should address:</p> <ul><li>physical safety and non-recurrence;</li> <li>housing, land, and property claims;</li> <li>documentation and nationality;</li> <li>restitution or compensation;</li> <li>livelihood and service access;</li> <li>family reunification;</li> <li>cultural and language continuity;</li> <li>disability and health needs;</li> <li>environmental restoration;</li> <li>monitoring and renewed flight risk.</li></ul> <h2>17.3 Rematriation</h2> <p>Indigenous rematriation is governed by the affected nation’s authority and valid land, treaty, restitution, or political arrangements. The framework supports claims and coordination but does not transfer governance or land itself.</p> <h2>17.4 Dual residence and circular mobility</h2> <p>Competent authorities may create dual-residence or circular-mobility arrangements. Participation shall not weaken labour rights, family rights, social protection, or the ability to change employer or status where law permits.</p> <hr> <h1>18. Work, livelihoods, and economic safeguards</h1> <h2>18.1 Work is not the price of protection</h2> <p>Shelter, protection, status procedures, healthcare, and basic support shall not depend on unpaid labour, ecological work, care work, public praise, or participation in Hearts, Leaves, or the Love Ledger.</p> <h2>18.2 Equal labour protection</h2> <p>Migrant workers shall receive lawful wages, safe conditions, organizing rights, complaint access, wage recovery, social protection, and protection from retaliation independent of status where possible.</p> <h2>18.3 Employer-tied status</h2> <p>Where status is linked to employment, law should provide safe employer-change, complaint, and transition routes so that status does not become a tool of coercion.</p> <h2>18.4 Recruitment and fees</h2> <p>Recruitment fees, document confiscation, deceptive contracts, debt bondage, retaliatory reporting, discriminatory deductions, and tied housing abuses require prevention, remedy, and where lawful, investigation through Shield v2.0.1 and Justice v1.4.</p> <h2>18.5 Informal work and recognition</h2> <p>Formalization pathways should protect workers rather than expose them to immediate detention, deportation, debt, or loss of livelihood.</p> <hr> <h1>19. Data, identity technology, and security firewalls</h1> <h2>19.1 Aurora and CIS control</h2> <p>Aurora Accord v1.3.2 controls personal, community, cultural, identity, biometric, location, health, family, and case-data purpose, minimization, anti-linkage, access, correction, retention, and secondary use. CIS/0.2.4 controls purpose-limited person-continuity assertions, non-transitivity, merge/split/collision correction, and external-status ingestion. Neither instrument creates migration, civil-registration, citizenship, border, or status authority.</p> <h2>19.2 No mandatory blockchain</h2> <p>No implementation requires blockchain, decentralized identity, Love Ledger, universal digital wallet, or global database. Paper, local, offline, federated, and human-readable alternatives shall be available.</p> <h2>19.3 Prohibited automated decisions</h2> <p>Automated systems shall not determine:</p> <ul><li>legal identity;</li> <li>nationality;</li> <li>credibility;</li> <li>asylum or exclusion;</li> <li>dangerousness;</li> <li>detention;</li> <li>family relationship;</li> <li>guardianship;</li> <li>trafficking status;</li> <li>deportation or extradition;</li> <li>access to essential services.</li></ul> <h2>19.4 Security access</h2> <p>Mobility data shall not flow into Shield, police, intelligence, military, border, employer, landlord, welfare-fraud, or political systems by default. Any exception requires a specific lawful basis, necessity, proportionality, minimization, access logging, review, and challenge where safe.</p> <h2>19.5 Peace, health, and survivor data</h2> <p>Information created for mediation, spiritual dialogue, trauma support, healthcare, shelter, truth processes, or community healing is presumptively unavailable for immigration enforcement or security classification.</p> <h2>19.6 Public dashboards</h2> <p>Public reporting shall use aggregate, disclosure-resistant data. It shall not expose routes, shelter locations, survivor identities, family links, irregular status, Indigenous protected information, or small-group characteristics that enable targeting.</p> <hr> <h1>20. Cross-framework interfaces</h1> <h2>20.1 Justice Systems v1.4.1</h2> <p>Justice governs standing, evidence, independent adjudication, detention review, appeals, remedies, and enforcement of lawful orders. Migration bodies cannot create their own tribunal or substitute an ombuds for judicial relief.</p> <h2>20.2 Shield Protocol v2.0.1</h2> <p>Shield may support trafficking, exploitation, document crime, corruption, and cross-border criminal cooperation. Mobility services remain separate from intelligence and investigation. Security allegations enter protection or removal processes only through SCPA-30.</p> <h2>20.3 Kintsugi Protocol v2.2.1</h2> <p>Kintsugi may provide unarmed care, de-escalation, survivor advocacy, sanctuary, and voluntary restoration. It cannot detain, search, investigate, or enforce migration status.</p> <h2>20.4 Peace and Conflict Resolution</h2> <p>Peace services may support host–migrant dialogue, ceasefires, return agreements, and conflict prevention. Values, spiritual, trauma, and mediation data cannot become migration or security classifications.</p> <h2>20.5 Living Shield and emergency interfaces</h2> <p>Living Shield may support evacuation, shelter, disaster assistance, and host infrastructure. Temporary evacuation does not determine permanent relocation or extinguish return rights. Emergency records remain distinct from SCPA mobility decisions.</p> <h2>20.6 Aegis</h2> <p>Converted civilian capabilities may support transport, communications, shelter construction, or disaster logistics under valid mission charters. Any use for border surveillance, identity screening, detention, deportation, raids, intelligence, targeting, or armed action triggers the Aegis security-use reversion gate and the relevant SCPA route.</p> <h2>20.7 Health systems</h2> <p>Health access is not immigration enforcement. Public-health measures require their own lawful basis and cannot become generalized movement control.</p> <h2>20.8 AUBI and Work in Liberation</h2> <p>Unconditional support remains unconditional. Hearts, Leaves, Reciprocity Capital, work credentials, or contribution records cannot determine mobility status, protection, or essential-service access.</p> <hr> <h1>21. Funding and solidarity</h1> <h2>21.1 Valid finance</h2> <p>Funding may come from public budgets, treaty-authorized funds, grants, philanthropy, insurance, development finance, reparations, or other lawful instruments. This framework creates no tax, levy, debt relief, credit priority, or fiscal entitlement.</p> <h2>21.2 Solidarity Support Account</h2> <p>The source’s Reciprocity Capital is replaced by a non-tradable <strong>Solidarity Support Account</strong> used for transparent planning and recognition of documented host costs and contributions. It does not create money, priority rights, sanctions, credit scores, or claims on future unrelated aid.</p> <h2>21.3 Allocation criteria</h2> <p>Funding should reflect need, rights, accessibility, host infrastructure, child and survivor protection, housing, public services, environmental impacts, and underfunded responsibilities. It shall not reward exclusion, rapid deportation, biometric enrolment, surveillance, low asylum recognition, or assimilation metrics.</p> <h2>21.4 No punishment for non-participation</h2> <p>A jurisdiction that declines a voluntary resettlement programme shall not be sanctioned by this framework. It remains responsible for duties arising under applicable law and may not discriminate against people already within its jurisdiction.</p> <h2>21.5 Legal Defence and Mobility Rights Fund</h2> <p>A separately constituted fund may support counsel, documentation, strategic litigation, detention review, family tracing, survivor advocacy, and remedies. It shall be independent of enforcement bodies and may not condition aid on publicity or test-case participation.</p> <hr> <h1>22. Accountability, remedies, and institutional closure</h1> <h2>22.1 Complaints</h2> <p>Accessible complaint routes shall cover service denial, discrimination, abuse, data misuse, unsafe accommodation, family separation, exploitation, detention, removal, identity errors, interpretation failure, disability exclusion, retaliation, and corruption.</p> <h2>22.2 Urgent relief</h2> <p>People facing imminent removal, detention, family separation, trafficking, homelessness, or serious harm shall have an urgent route to competent review and interim relief.</p> <h2>22.3 Remedies</h2> <p>Available remedies should include admission to procedure, release, corrected status or records, reunification, restored services, returned documents or property, deleted or restricted data, compensation, public correction, disciplinary referral, contract termination, and institutional reform.</p> <h2>22.4 No retaliation</h2> <p>Complaints, appeals, biometric refusal, union activity, reporting exploitation, refusing mediation, or declining publicity shall not reduce services or trigger security action.</p> <h2>22.5 Closure and succession</h2> <p>When a reception centre, casework service, corridor, fund, pilot, digital system, or mobility network closes, a continuity plan shall preserve active cases, legal deadlines, child and survivor responsibilities, property, wages, data disposition, complaint routes, and unresolved duties. Performance does not create permanent jurisdiction.</p> <hr> <h1>23. Implementation and Genesis alignment</h1> <h2>23.1 Permissible pilots</h2> <p>Pre-constitutional pilots may test:</p> <ul><li>voluntary reception and service coordination;</li> <li>non-biometric service credentials;</li> <li>legal orientation and casework;</li> <li>family tracing with consent;</li> <li>accessible shelter and host infrastructure;</li> <li>voluntary host–migrant compacts;</li> <li>anti-exploitation services;</li> <li>voluntary return counselling;</li> <li>aggregate learning and privacy-preserving interoperability.</li></ul> <h2>23.2 Prohibited pilots</h2> <p>Pilots shall not test on real people without lawful authority:</p> <ul><li>compulsory biometrics;</li> <li>automated status or credibility decisions;</li> <li>detention, deportation, extradition, border interception, or forced relocation;</li> <li>hidden intelligence sharing;</li> <li>universal identity systems;</li> <li>framework-created protected zones or corridors;</li> <li>sanctions or loss of essential services for non-participation;</li> <li>coercive labour or assimilation targets.</li></ul> <h2>23.3 First-seating gate</h2> <p>Before consequential implementation, the responsible jurisdiction shall demonstrate valid law, independent review, counsel and interpretation, data safeguards, accessibility, child and survivor protections, non-refoulement, detention safeguards, remedy, and institutional closure plans.</p> <h2>23.4 Version control</h2> <p>Implementations shall pin the versions of Migration v1.1.2, SCPA v0.1, Justice v1.4.1, CIS v0.2.4, Aurora v1.3.2, and any applicable emergency, Shield, Kintsugi, Indigenous, health, or labour instruments.</p> <p>For security/coercion cluster routes, the exact SCCA/0.1 registry remains controlling until explicitly updated. Because SCCA/0.1 tested Migration v1.1.1, an implementation must not claim that v1.1.2 is an exact tested SCCA member merely from this framework revision. Until a compatibility registry update is published, v1.1.2 implementations using those routes must preserve the v1.1.1 security/coercion semantics without modification and record the compatibility exception.</p> <hr> <h1>24. Monitoring, evaluation, and anti-Goodhart safeguards</h1> <h2>24.1 Appropriate measures</h2> <p>Useful measures include:</p> <ul><li>time to essential services;</li> <li>legal-representation access;</li> <li>interpretation and accessibility coverage;</li> <li>family tracing and safe reunification;</li> <li>housing stability;</li> <li>education and healthcare continuity;</li> <li>wage recovery and recruitment-abuse remedy;</li> <li>detention duration and release outcomes;</li> <li>appeal access and correction rates;</li> <li>non-refoulement compliance;</li> <li>host-infrastructure adequacy;</li> <li>complaint resolution and compensation;</li> <li>voluntary return sustainability;</li> <li>data deletion and correction performance.</li></ul> <h2>24.2 Prohibited targets</h2> <p>No target shall be set for:</p> <ul><li>deportations, removals, detention occupancy, biometric enrolment, asylum refusal, fraud findings, intelligence referrals, assimilation, cultural conformity, reconciliation, reduced reporting, or public approval;</li> <li>percentage of migrants earning Hearts or performing designated work;</li> <li>host-community acceptance as a condition of individual rights;</li> <li>speed at the expense of accuracy, counsel, appeal, or safety.</li></ul> <h2>24.3 Qualitative learning</h2> <p>Evaluation shall include migrant, host, Indigenous, survivor, child, disabled-person, worker, and frontline-provider testimony without exposing individuals or converting participation into eligibility.</p> <h2>24.4 Failure library</h2> <p>Implementations should document wrongful detention, failed reception, unsafe return, family separation, data misuse, inaccessible services, host-capacity failures, exploitation, and false security allegations. Learning records shall not become individual watchlists.</p> <hr> <h1>Appendix A — Operational record register</h1> <table><thead><tr><th>ID</th><th>Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>MOB-01</td><td>Mobility Framework Authority and Version Record</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-02</td><td>Service Access and Minimum Attestation Record</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-03</td><td>Identity Function-Separation and Credential Record</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-04</td><td>Identity Reconstruction and Evidence Record</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-05</td><td>Biometric Choice, Necessity, and Alternative Record</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-06</td><td>Asylum or Protection Application Record</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-07</td><td>Temporary Admission or Protection Record</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-08</td><td>Non-Refoulement Assessment Record</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-09</td><td>Country, Territory, and Route Information Record</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-10</td><td>Security Allegation Firewall and Disclosure Record</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-11</td><td>Counsel, Interpretation, Accessibility, and Notice Record</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-12</td><td>Family Unity and Tracing Record</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-13</td><td>Child or Dependent-Person Best-Interests Record</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-14</td><td>Independent Guardian Appointment and Conflict Record</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-15</td><td>Reception Placement, Consent, and Conditions Record</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-16</td><td>Reception Exit, Movement, and Non-Custody Record</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-17</td><td>Health, Disability, Trauma, and Support Needs Record</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-18</td><td>Trafficking, Exploitation, and Survivor Choice Record</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-19</td><td>Detention Presumption, Alternatives, and Review Record</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-20</td><td>Detention Conditions, Welfare, and Release Record</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-21</td><td>Expulsion or Deportation Decision Record</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-22</td><td>Extradition or Criminal Transfer Interface Record</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-23</td><td>Voluntary Return and Reintegration Record</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-24</td><td>Right-to-Return, Property, and Rematriation Claim Record</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-25</td><td>Planned Relocation Offer and No-Forced-Movement Record</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-26</td><td>Climate and Disaster Mobility Assessment Record</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-27</td><td>Mobility Corridor, Protected Route, or Zone Authority Record</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-28</td><td>Host–Migrant Co-Governance Compact Record</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-29</td><td>Affected Indigenous Nation and Territory Interface Record</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-30</td><td>Host Capacity, Infrastructure, and Third-Space Plan</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-31</td><td>Work, Recruitment, Wage, and Portability Protection Record</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-32</td><td>Legal Defence, Complaint, Appeal, and Interim Relief Record</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-33</td><td>Mobility Data Purpose, Access, Retention, and Deletion Record</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-34</td><td>Cross-Framework Handoff and Acceptance Record</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-35</td><td>Public Communication, Correction, and Anti-Stigmatization Record</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-36</td><td>Funding, Solidarity Support, and Non-Conditionality Record</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-37</td><td>Pilot Authority and Non-Activation Record</td></tr><tr><td>MOB-38</td><td>Closure, Handback, Data Disposition, and Unresolved Duty Record</td></tr></tbody></table> <p>Where person continuity is consequential, <strong>CIS/0.2.4 Appendix M</strong> is embedded as applicable in MOB-02, MOB-03, MOB-04, MOB-12, MOB-13, MOB-24, MOB-33, MOB-34, or MOB-38 rather than creating a universal Migration identity record.</p> <p>Records specialize but do not replace SCPA records. Missing mapping defaults to direct canonical import, not omission.</p> <hr> <h1>Appendix B — SCPA v0.1 conformance crosswalk</h1> <table><thead><tr><th>SCPA ID</th><th>Canonical record</th><th>Migration v1.1.1 relationship</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>SCPA-01</td><td>Protective or Coercive Action Authority Provenance Record</td><td>MOB-01 implements</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-02</td><td>Standing, Jurisdiction, and Territorial Basis Map</td><td>MOB-01/MOB-27/MOB-29 split</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-03</td><td>Action Classification and Procedural Route Record</td><td>MOB-34 extends</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-04</td><td>Individualized Factual Basis and Attribution Record</td><td>MOB-04/MOB-06/MOB-10 extend</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-05</td><td>Prohibited Inference and Automated-Use Record</td><td>MOB-05/MOB-10/MOB-33 extend</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-06</td><td>Necessity, Proportionality, and Alternatives Record</td><td>MOB-19/MOB-21/MOB-25 extend</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-07</td><td>Rights, Accessibility, and Protected-Status Safeguards Record</td><td>MOB-11/MOB-13/MOB-17/MOB-18 extend</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-08</td><td>Notice, Reasons, Counsel, Interpretation, and Challenge Record</td><td>MOB-11/MOB-32 split</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-09</td><td>Protected Information or Source Intake Record</td><td>MOB-09/MOB-18 extend</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-10</td><td>Intelligence Purpose, Minimization, and Dissemination Record</td><td>MOB-10/MOB-33 extend</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-11</td><td>Investigation Opening and Mandate Record</td><td>imports when investigation opens</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-12</td><td>Investigation Method, Scope, and Minimization Record</td><td>imports when investigative methods used</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-13</td><td>Compulsory Process Application Record</td><td>imports for compulsory process</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-14</td><td>Search, Entry, Inspection, or Access Authorization Record</td><td>imports for search/entry/access</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-15</td><td>Disclosure, Production, Preservation, or Interception Order Record</td><td>imports for disclosure/preservation/interception</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-16</td><td>Evidence Seizure, Copying, and Chain-of-Custody Record</td><td>imports for evidence seizure</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-17</td><td>Asset Preservation, Freeze, Restraint, or Seizure Record</td><td>imports for asset restraint</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-18</td><td>Urgent Protective Action Record</td><td>MOB-07/MOB-18 extend for urgent protection</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-19</td><td>Arrest or Physical Restraint Record</td><td>imports for arrest/restraint</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-20</td><td>Custody or Detention Authority and Welfare Record</td><td>MOB-19/MOB-20 extend</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-21</td><td>Release, Bail, Supervision, or Less-Restrictive Alternative Record</td><td>MOB-19/MOB-20 extend</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-22</td><td>Transfer, Extradition, Removal, or Return Authority Record</td><td>MOB-21/MOB-22/MOB-23 split</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-23</td><td>Use-of-Force Incident and Necessity Record</td><td>imports for force</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-24</td><td>Weapons, Restraint Device, and Special Equipment Authorization Record</td><td>imports for weapons/equipment</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-25</td><td>Mission Command, Technical Command, Custody, and Stop-Work Record</td><td>MOB-27/MOB-34 extend for mission/route command</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-26</td><td>Defensive Cyber Action Record</td><td>imports for defensive cyber action</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-27</td><td>Third-Party Cyber Access, Disruption, or Counter-Operation Record</td><td>imports for third-party cyber action</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-28</td><td>Financial Coercion, Sanction, Freeze, Forfeiture, or Exclusion Record</td><td>imports for sanctions or financial coercion</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-29</td><td>Cross-Border Cooperation and Mutual Legal Assistance Record</td><td>MOB-22/MOB-27/MOB-34 extend</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-30</td><td>Asylum, Protection, Non-Refoulement, and Security-Allegation Record</td><td>MOB-06/MOB-07/MOB-08/MOB-10 implement</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-31</td><td>Independent Authorization, Adjudication, or Review Record</td><td>MOB-08/MOB-13/MOB-19/MOB-21/MOB-22 extend</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-32</td><td>Appeal, Complaint, Urgent Challenge, and Interim Relief Record</td><td>MOB-32 implements</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-33</td><td>Remedy, Restitution, Property Return, and Compensation Record</td><td>MOB-24/MOB-32/MOB-38 extend</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-34</td><td>Restorative, Rehabilitation, Correctional, or Reintegration Consent and Suitability Record</td><td>MOB-23 extends; otherwise imports</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-35</td><td>Security Data Disposition, Correction, Expungement, and Use-Limitation Record</td><td>MOB-33/MOB-38 implement</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-36</td><td>Action Termination, Authority Return, and Unresolved-Risk Custody Record</td><td>MOB-38 implements</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-37</td><td>Identity, Credential, and Service-Access Function Separation Record</td><td>MOB-02/MOB-03/MOB-04/MOB-05 implement</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-38</td><td>Child, Dependent Person, Guardianship, and Best-Interests Record</td><td>MOB-12/MOB-13/MOB-14 implement</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-39</td><td>Indigenous Territory, Authority, Knowledge, and Affected-Nation Interface Record</td><td>MOB-24/MOB-29 implement</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-40</td><td>Survivor Safety, Confidentiality, and Process Choice Record</td><td>MOB-18 implements</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-41</td><td>Worker, Operator, Responder, and Whistleblower Protection Record</td><td>MOB-31 extends</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-42</td><td>Public Communication, Classification, Correction, and Non-Propaganda Record</td><td>MOB-35 implements</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-43</td><td>Security Institution Transfer, Succession, or Dissolution Continuity Plan</td><td>MOB-38 extends</td></tr><tr><td>SCPA-44</td><td>Coercive-Power Non-Activation and Security-Use Reversion Register</td><td>MOB-37 implements and imports Aegis reversion</td></tr></tbody></table> <hr> <h1>Appendix C — Constitutional readiness gates</h1> <p>An implementation passes only when all applicable gates are satisfied.</p> <ol><li><strong>Authority provenance:</strong> every consequential act identifies valid law and competent authority.</li> <li><strong>Function separation:</strong> service access, identity, asylum, benefits, health, child protection, and security records remain distinct.</li> <li><strong>No documentation barrier:</strong> essential services remain available without compulsory identity completion.</li> <li><strong>Biometric alternative:</strong> a rights-equivalent non-biometric path exists where feasible.</li> <li><strong>Asylum access:</strong> protection claims can be made safely and promptly.</li> <li><strong>Non-refoulement:</strong> return-to-harm assessment and suspensive relief exist.</li> <li><strong>Security firewall:</strong> allegations cannot silently determine status, detention, or removal.</li> <li><strong>Counsel and interpretation:</strong> meaningful access is operational.</li> <li><strong>Child safeguards:</strong> independent representation, best interests, and no migration detention are operational.</li> <li><strong>Family unity:</strong> tracing and safe reunification procedures exist.</li> <li><strong>Survivor sovereignty:</strong> trafficking and abuse support is not conditioned on prosecution.</li> <li><strong>Disability access:</strong> procedures and facilities are accessible.</li> <li><strong>Liberty:</strong> detention is exceptional, individualized, reviewable, and time-bounded.</li> <li><strong>Alternatives:</strong> less restrictive measures are funded and available.</li> <li><strong>Removal procedure:</strong> expulsion, deportation, extradition, transfer, and voluntary return are separated.</li> <li><strong>Territorial authority:</strong> corridors, zones, camps, and relocation sites have valid legal and land authority.</li> <li><strong>Indigenous authority:</strong> affected-nation approval and protected knowledge rules are operational.</li> <li><strong>Host capacity:</strong> infrastructure and remedy plans exist without exclusionary scoring.</li> <li><strong>Labour rights:</strong> recruitment, wages, document custody, and employer-change safeguards exist.</li> <li><strong>Data minimization:</strong> access, retention, correction, deletion, and security-use limits are implemented.</li> <li><strong>No automated legal consequence:</strong> humans with lawful authority decide status and coercive actions.</li> <li><strong>Remedy:</strong> urgent challenge, appeal, correction, release, compensation, and property return exist.</li> <li><strong>Closure:</strong> active cases, data, funds, property, and duties survive institutional closure safely.</li> <li><strong>Pilot boundary:</strong> no coercive, identity, detention, removal, or forced-relocation power is tested without valid law.</li> <li><strong>Person continuity:</strong> any cross-domain same-person recognition is purpose-limited, separately authorized, source-preserving, non-transitive, correctable, and unlinkable.</li> <li><strong>External status:</strong> death or other civil-status events are consumed only from competent external sources and do not erase active claims, remedies, or unresolved duties.</li></ol> <hr> <h1>Appendix D — Compound implementation tests</h1> <ol><li>Undocumented person requests food and shelter while refusing biometric enrolment. <strong>Pass condition:</strong> assistance continues through minimum attestation.</li> <li>Asylum seeker presents inconsistent dates after trauma. <strong>Pass condition:</strong> no automatic credibility rejection; accessible evidence process.</li> <li>Shield sends a secret allegation about an applicant. <strong>Pass condition:</strong> SCPA-30 firewall, independent review, challenge or lawful substitute, no automatic exclusion.</li> <li>Host community votes to deny healthcare to new arrivals. <strong>Pass condition:</strong> vote cannot override essential-service rights.</li> <li>Category-five storm triggers displacement warning. <strong>Pass condition:</strong> assessment and assistance activate; no automatic visa or permanent relocation by metric.</li> <li>Reception centre locks exits “for safety.” <strong>Pass condition:</strong> classified as custody; SCPA Class F records and independent review required.</li> <li>Child arrives without documents and with an unrelated adult. <strong>Pass condition:</strong> independent guardian and best-interests assessment; no automatic family assumption.</li> <li>Trafficking survivor declines police cooperation. <strong>Pass condition:</strong> shelter, healthcare, legal aid, and protection continue.</li> <li>Employer threatens to report a worker’s status. <strong>Pass condition:</strong> safe complaint and employer-change route; no retaliation.</li> <li>Authority proposes deportation to persecution. <strong>Pass condition:</strong> non-refoulement review and suspensive appeal block removal.</li> <li>Extradition request concerns conduct framed as political crime. <strong>Pass condition:</strong> separate extradition and protection procedures.</li> <li>A person agrees to assisted return because detention conditions are intolerable. <strong>Pass condition:</strong> consent treated as potentially coerced; release and independent counselling.</li> <li>Community attestation later proves partly inaccurate. <strong>Pass condition:</strong> correction without collective punishment or automatic fraud finding.</li> <li>Biometric system produces a false match. <strong>Pass condition:</strong> no legal consequence without human review; correction and expungement.</li> <li>Family tracing identifies an alleged abuser. <strong>Pass condition:</strong> survivor and child safety override automatic reunification.</li> <li>Indigenous nation refuses a relocation site on its territory. <strong>Pass condition:</strong> framework cannot override; alternative planning required.</li> <li>Emergency evacuation uses Aegis aircraft. <strong>Pass condition:</strong> civilian mission only; no border surveillance or screening without reversion and SCPA authority.</li> <li>Host infrastructure is overloaded. <strong>Pass condition:</strong> transparent capacity support and lawful placement adjustments; no collective rights suspension.</li> <li>Health service is asked to report undocumented patients. <strong>Pass condition:</strong> health–enforcement firewall unless a specific narrow legal exception applies.</li> <li>Mediation participant admits irregular entry. <strong>Pass condition:</strong> mediation confidentiality prevents silent immigration referral.</li> <li>Reception contractor closes suddenly. <strong>Pass condition:</strong> continuity plan preserves shelter, active cases, wages, property, and data.</li> <li>Government labels an area uninhabitable based on one model. <strong>Pass condition:</strong> plural assessment, uncertainty, adaptation options, and no forced relocation.</li> <li>Child turns eighteen during a protection case. <strong>Pass condition:</strong> continuity and transition plan; no abrupt loss of shelter or counsel.</li> <li>Stateless person cannot prove nationality. <strong>Pass condition:</strong> service access and protection continue; identity uncertainty is recorded rather than invented.</li> <li>Public dashboard could identify a small refugee family. <strong>Pass condition:</strong> disclosure-risk suppression.</li> <li>Host fund rewards “successful integration.” <strong>Pass condition:</strong> no assimilation or conformity metric; funding based on services and rights.</li> <li>Return area becomes unsafe again. <strong>Pass condition:</strong> renewed protection access and no penalty for re-displacement.</li> <li>Corridor authority expires while people remain in transit. <strong>Pass condition:</strong> continuity and safe handoff without stranded status.</li> <li>Migrant refuses Love Ledger participation. <strong>Pass condition:</strong> no loss of service, work rights, benefits, or protection.</li> <li>Wrongful detention is established. <strong>Pass condition:</strong> release, record correction, compensation, investigation of systemic cause, and no retaliation.</li> <li>Migration and Housing records are mistakenly linked to the same person. <strong>Pass condition:</strong> correction without rewriting the Housing source record; downstream recipients receive only authorized corrections; unrelated linked systems do not gain access.</li> <li>A competent civil authority confirms an applicant died while an appeal, wage claim, family-tracing duty, and property-return claim remain open. <strong>Pass condition:</strong> Migration updates only its own status, preserves surviving claims/duties, and refers succession/substitution questions to competent law rather than closing everything automatically.</li></ol> <hr> <h1>Appendix E — Source and compatibility manifest</h1> <table><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>File</th><th>SHA-256</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Migration & Human Mobility v1.0</td><td><code>migration-human-mobility-framework-v1.0.md</code></td><td><code>87d2a27023e9f450bc892e67ae34c311421891e220fb587ee05b0dcd53a28b84</code></td></tr><tr><td>Security, Coercion, and Protective Action Interface Specification v0.1</td><td><code>security-coercion-protective-action-interface-specification-v0.1.md</code></td><td><code>b8f1dd0f676044542a23184f52200e50b106a798545c7c57119ded2c439b6299</code></td></tr><tr><td>Security and Coercion Cluster Constitutional Audit v0.1</td><td><code>security-coercion-cluster-constitutional-audit-v0.1.md</code></td><td><code>e4a364534136bd7fba9e6c6f46d982647709dc91598265afff8e122fb3180b10</code></td></tr><tr><td>Justice Systems Framework v1.4.1</td><td><code>justice-systems-v1.4.1.md</code></td><td><code>4f64b501563d257397a2bbf6ec3e649f7d3b4e44754ff18c99e329be3f4068c8</code></td></tr><tr><td>Constitutional Interface Specification v0.2.4</td><td><code>constitutional-interface-specification-v0.2.4.md</code></td><td><code>77fe1742e7e9698febc06b3bb3e6ddb595e5933ac222a6113c432e8cb62c7f46</code></td></tr><tr><td>Aurora Accord v1.3.2</td><td><code>aurora-accord-v1.3.2.md</code></td><td><code>a27c8b3b1ae3e88dfe03e88d604695ac7f37d88f9f69e8e3bf57e44eb1622e27</code></td></tr><tr><td>Shield Protocol v2.0</td><td><code>shield-protocol-v2.0.md</code></td><td><code>4fca33e9ab55574b3255fc36c0a5e81dedd5f2ef6db5f0df3f5887308fbc2b62</code></td></tr><tr><td>Kintsugi Protocol v2.2</td><td><code>kintsugi-protocol-v2.2.md</code></td><td><code>35f06e1449df25c5077c6b7c7b9d6c23f39c499df3d78007951778e91d96c973</code></td></tr><tr><td>Emergency Interface Specification v0.1</td><td><code>emergency-catastrophic-risk-continuity-interface-specification-v0.1.md</code></td><td><code>c673742b1317c7169d4b8226118478c990e9a3d05084b2dc5ac9ad2f1971163c</code></td></tr><tr><td>Living Shield v4.4.2</td><td><code>living-shield-v4.4.2.md</code></td><td><code>64921d6a7fab2f877259ccdc8370b103ddbd2d240fb1d55c9bfae66ea03ff161</code></td></tr><tr><td>Genesis Protocol v0.9</td><td><code>genesis-protocol-v0.9.md</code></td><td><code>3af8dfe6c90be0f55e80d2c67503440e98f672369f5c5638fd41a80411fa5d49</code></td></tr></tbody></table> <p>The source v1.0 remains historically relevant but is not implementation-compatible with v1.1 where it creates automatic status, mandatory biometrics, universal credentials, framework-created zones, or untyped coercive consequences.</p> <hr> <hr> <h1>Appendix F — Cluster harmonization adoption — controlled patch v1.1.1</h1> <h2>Normative cluster adoption</h2> <p>This version is a controlled conformance patch. It changes interface adoption, record inheritance, transport, cross-framework responsibility, emergency linkage, pairwise specialization, and exact release compatibility. It does not add a police, intelligence, investigation, search, custody, force, cyber, border, sanctions, prosecutorial, adjudicative, military, or emergency power.</p> <p>It normatively adopts:</p> <ul><li><code>SCPA-RIC/0.1</code>, SHA-256 <code>0c5d6083716969ee499afc5027a812f7153410babdd4a8e6f464270f8534c917</code>;</li> <li><code>CFPAE/0.1</code>, SHA-256 <code>c1d591c665dfd3b9c9f30dd61943a38556905c6f1a2128a64220e054149dfb05</code>;</li> <li><code>ESDIB/0.1</code>, SHA-256 <code>6332dba2fe2d01347f5c2432a1af002e0902aa8bba80c7b533bd50ee89cebf30</code>;</li> <li>the exact security-and-coercion cluster release set <code>SCCA/0.1</code>.</li></ul> <p>The following precedence applies only within their functions:</p> <ol><li><code>SCPA/0.1</code> controls action classification, authority separation, safeguards, review, termination, and remedy;</li> <li><code>SCPA-RIC/0.1</code> controls canonical record identity, the inherited 27-field header, access classes, handoff states, lifecycle states, and <code>implements</code> / <code>extends</code> / <code>splits</code> / <code>contains</code> / <code>imports</code> / <code>not_applicable</code> semantics;</li> <li><code>CFPAE/0.1</code> controls compound protective-action identity, topology, explicit acceptance, concurrent responsibility, custody, independent clocks, correction, remedy, and closure;</li> <li><code>ESDIB/0.1</code> controls dual emergency–security linkage without authority, command, clock, or custody merger;</li> <li>an adopted pairwise addendum controls only its named cross-framework route and cannot expand either framework’s powers;</li> <li>this framework continues to control its substantive non-authority, services, programme design, and domain-specific duties.</li></ol> <h2>Mandatory record inheritance</h2> <p>Every domain record that implements, extends, splits, contains, or imports an <code>SCPA-01</code>–<code>SCPA-44</code> record SHALL preserve the canonical identifier, exact SCPA version, full inherited header, access class, linked-record topology, review and expiry clocks, correction history, termination duty, remedy duty, and data-disposition duty. Combining forms or transporting several records in one packet does not combine authorities.</p> <p>An unmapped or incompatible record fails closed. <code>not_applicable</code> requires an action-specific reasoned determination and cannot mean unknown, inconvenient, unsupported by software, deferred, or unfunded.</p> <h2>Mandatory compound-action envelope</h2> <p>A <code>CFPAE/0.1</code> envelope SHALL be instantiated when two or more frameworks, authorities, jurisdictions, action routes, or custody systems coordinate one protective action. The immutable <code>protective_action_cluster_id</code> links the routes but creates no common jurisdiction or command.</p> <p>Each route SHALL retain its own competent authority, legal source, jurisdiction, exact act, SCPA class, record instances, handoff state, affected-person rights, review clock, expiry, custody, correction, remedy, and closure condition. Silence is not acceptance. Partial acceptance transfers only the named function and material. Sender duties survive unless valid law and an accepted record expressly reassign them.</p> <h2>Emergency–security dual interface</h2> <p>An <code>ESDIB/0.1</code> bridge SHALL be instantiated whenever an emergency event and an SCPA-governed action route materially depend on one another. CDEE/ECRC records may establish emergency context, event identity, logistics, or temporary coordination; they cannot satisfy an SCPA authority requirement. SCPA records may authorize a security or protective act; they cannot declare or prolong an emergency.</p> <p>Where an act requires both bases, both remain valid and separately reviewable, and the earlier expiry controls. Continuation after one basis ends requires a recorded detachment to ordinary law and removal of the expired basis’s command, credentials, data access, finance, privileges, and operational dependencies.</p> <h2>Exact release compatibility</h2> <p><strong>v1.1.2 does not silently amend the tested SCCA/0.1 release set.</strong> The security/coercion cluster was tested with Migration v1.1.1. The person-continuity additions in v1.1.2 are outside SCPA/CFPAE/ESDIB action authority and intentionally preserve the v1.1.1 security-route semantics. Until <code>security-coercion-cluster-conformance-registry-v0.1.json</code> or a successor registry explicitly lists v1.1.2, implementations shall treat v1.1.1 as the exact tested Migration member for SCCA compatibility and record that v1.1.2 is an unregistered additive successor.</p> <p>The tested release set remains:</p> <table><thead><tr><th>Instrument</th><th align="right">Exact version in release set</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Security, Coercion, and Protective Action Interface</td><td align="right"><code>SCPA/0.1</code></td></tr><tr><td>SCPA Record Inheritance and Conformance Clause</td><td align="right"><code>SCPA-RIC/0.1</code></td></tr><tr><td>Cross-Framework Protective Action Envelope</td><td align="right"><code>CFPAE/0.1</code></td></tr><tr><td>Emergency–Security Dual-Interface Bridge</td><td align="right"><code>ESDIB/0.1</code></td></tr><tr><td>Shield–Migration Addendum</td><td align="right"><code>SATN/0.1</code></td></tr><tr><td>Peace–Kintsugi–Shield Addendum</td><td align="right"><code>PKS-PRUP/0.1</code></td></tr><tr><td>Shield Protocol</td><td align="right"><code>2.0.1</code></td></tr><tr><td>Kintsugi Protocol</td><td align="right"><code>2.2.1</code></td></tr><tr><td>Migration and Human Mobility Framework</td><td align="right"><code>1.1.1</code></td></tr><tr><td>Peace and Conflict Resolution Framework</td><td align="right"><code>1.8.1</code></td></tr><tr><td>Aegis Protocol</td><td align="right"><code>1.3.4</code></td></tr></tbody></table> <p>Exact member hashes, source hashes, schemas, record mappings, trigger rules, and closure requirements are published in <code>security-coercion-cluster-conformance-registry-v0.1.json</code>. A later version, similar title, local fork, or apparently protective amendment is not automatically compatible. Material mismatch blocks the affected cross-framework route pending explicit compatibility review and a recorded manifest update.</p> <p>Accordingly, the <code>Migration and Human Mobility Framework | 1.1.1</code> row above remains intentionally unchanged until the conformance registry is updated. This v1.1.2 document may be adopted for its non-security person-continuity rules without representing that the exact SCCA member has changed.</p> <h2>Closure and reopening</h2> <p>A compound route cannot close while authority return, release, property or credential return, pending remedy, correction, claims, data disposition, service continuity, Aegis requalification, or unresolved custody remains open. Reopening restores coordination only; it does not reactivate an expired warrant, detention, force authorization, surveillance permission, cyber action, freeze, sanction, transfer, removal order, or emergency power.</p> <h2>Migration-specific pairwise adoption</h2> <p>Migration adopts <code>SATN/0.1</code>, SHA-256 <code>79648d5c3ea4322e4978c04ea1f316f42c4a9aed2455aa52ea4a795c5bef6fdf</code>. A Shield security allegation may affect a protection or mobility proceeding only through a complete <code>SCPA-30</code> package with provenance, exact allegation, reliability limits, disclosure or effective substitute, independent review, answer rights, expiry, correction, and remedy.</p> <p>A trafficking indicator, irregular route, missing document, biometric refusal, intelligence match, or pending Shield review does not activate detention, family separation, exclusion, extradition, deportation, or return. Non-refoulement remains separately assessed and any challenge involving plausible irreversible harm has suspensive effect.</p> <h1>Conclusion</h1> <p>Human mobility governance is most legitimate when it reduces the power of missing documents, emergency labels, security allegations, employers, traffickers, borders, and institutions to turn vulnerability into coercion.</p> <p>The framework’s purpose is not to make every person legible to a planetary system. It is to make protection, lawful procedure, community capacity, and meaningful choice available even when identity is uncertain, institutions disagree, movement is dangerous, or return is impossible.</p> <blockquote><p><strong>A humane mobility system does not ask whether a person has earned dignity. It asks which authority may act, which right is at stake, which evidence is sufficient, which alternative is less restrictive, and how any power ends.</strong></p></blockquote>',1);function c(e){var t=o();n(838),r(e,t)}export{c as default};
